    "problem_statement": "# Callable default functions with keyword arguments not working properly\n\n## Description\n\nI'm trying to use callable default functions with keyword arguments in my schema, but it's not working as expected. When I pass keyword arguments to the validate method, they don't seem to be properly passed to the callable default functions.\n\n## How to Reproduce\n\nHere's a simple example that demonstrates the issue:\n\n```python\nfrom schema import Schema, Optional\n\ndef default_func(**kwargs):\n    return f\"Hello, {kwargs.get('name', 'Unknown')}\"\n\n# Define a schema with a callable default\nschema = Schema({Optional('greeting', default=default_func): str})\n\n# This doesn't work properly\nresult = schema.validate({}, name=\"World\")\nprint(result)  # Expected: {'greeting': 'Hello, World'}, but getting TypeError\n```\n\nI'm also seeing issues when using callable defaults in nested schemas:\n\n```python\nfrom schema import Schema, Optional\n\ndef convert(value, increment):\n    if isinstance(value, int):\n        return value + increment\n    return value\n\n# Define a schema with nested callable defaults\nschema = Schema({\n    'outer_key': int,\n    'nested': {\n        Optional('optional_key', default=lambda **kw: convert(5, kw['increment'])): int\n    }\n})\n\n# This doesn't work properly\ndata = {'outer_key': 10, 'nested': {}}\nresult = schema.validate(data, increment=2)\nprint(result)  # Expected to have 'optional_key': 7 in the nested dict\n```\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nKeyword arguments passed to the `validate()` method should be properly forwarded to callable default functions, allowing them to use these arguments in their logic.\n\n## Actual Behavior\n\nThe callable default functions are being called incorrectly, resulting in TypeError exceptions or incorrect default values being used."
